# Two wrappers for the schwa, which is a phoneme of Catalan's own and so has
# none of IBM's. Both are the shape of the 1,037 the module already has: bare,
# so there is no landing place and no ventproc, because a wrapper is entered
# and answers and the choice points around the call belong to whoever planted
# them.
#
# The two they stand beside are ZZtest_string_s_4_1_ZZstring115 and
# ZZlprp_load__insert_2pt_s_4_1_ZZstring115, which carry the phone a; the 4
# is the field the string belongs to and the 1 is how many.

# Whether the phone the scan is on is the schwa.
rule cat_test_schwa takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_schwa
end

# Lay a schwa down between the two ends a letter rule was handed.
rule cat_insert_schwa takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_schwa 0
end


# ---- the two open vowels -------------------------------------------------
#
# Getting a grave accent to a rule of its own takes three wrappers, because
# the module has nowhere for one to go. normalize_span_letters flattens every
# character Castilian does not use, and the letter chain has no arm for one
# that survives, so a grave either loses its accent or goes silent. Both are
# reached through wrappers used in exactly one place each, which is what makes
# this cheap: a wrapper is a rule, and a rule of ours in its place is an arm
# taken out of one chain and put into another.

# The two arms of the normaliser that flatten e and o with a grave. Answering
# 1 is what test_string_s answers when the character is not the one asked
# about, so the arm is never taken and the grave comes through as itself.
rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring164 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end

rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring9 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end

# And the arm of the letter chain that Castilian keeps for n with a tilde,
# which Catalan never writes: it spells that sound ny, and apply_span_n_rules
# is where that is done. So the arm is shared, and this is what it now asks
# -- is the letter either grave, or the tilde after all, or the raised dot of
# the geminate l? -- with apply_span_enye_rules, which it calls, rewritten to
# say which. The tilde is back because Spanish names come through Catalan
# text, and a Peña with a silent hole in it was heard.
rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring58 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_egrave
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_ograve
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_enye
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_bigenye
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_middot
end

# The open vowels as phones, for the value chain to test and a letter rule to
# lay down. The same shape as the schwa's pair.
rule cat_test_eopen takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_eopen
end

rule cat_test_oopen takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_oopen
end

rule cat_insert_eopen takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_eopen 0
end

rule cat_insert_oopen takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_oopen 0
end

# Whether the letter the scan is on is one Catalan says without voice. A
# stop before one of these devoices exactly as it does at the end of a word --
# dissabte is [d i s a p t @], dubte [d u p t @], capser [k a p s e] -- and
# the three stop rules ask this once rather than carrying five tests each.
#
# The caller sets the scan; a test that fails leaves it where it was, so the
# five can be asked one after another. Answering 0 is a match, which is what
# test_string_s answers and what the rules that call this read.
rule cat_voiceless_follows takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring104                 # t
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # s
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring20                  # c
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring90                  # p
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring48                  # f
end

# Nothing, laid down where Castilian lays down a joiner.
#
# Castilian writes a two-digit number as three words -- cuarenta y cinco --
# and convert_span_double puts the space and the y in between with two calls.
# Catalan writes quaranta-cinc and says it as one word with the stress on the
# unit, so both call sites in that rule are pointed at these and lay down
# nothing: the tens and the unit run together, and the accent the unit
# carries is what puts the stress on it. rules/constants says what the two
# forms sound like and which was asked for.
rule cat_insert_nothing takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 0
end

rule cat_insert_nothing_given takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 0
end

# A test that never matches, for an arm of Castilian's that Catalan does not
# take: the three hundreds it says with a word of their own, quinientos,
# setecientos and novecientos, are cinc-cents, set-cents and nou-cents here,
# which the regular arm says. Answering 1 is what test_string_s answers when
# the character is not the one asked about.
rule cat_never takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end


# ---- the arm that made an ll into a y ------------------------------------

# Castilian is yeista: its ll is /j/ and between two vowels it is the
# approximant, so span_phrase_level_rules has an arm that rewrites the phone L
# into y when the word before ends in a sonorant. Catalan's ll is a lateral
# wherever it stands -- la llum is [l @ L u m] and not [l @ y u m], el llop
# [@ l L O p] -- and that arm was making it a y at every word boundary after a
# vowel, which is most of the times the language writes one.
#
# The arm is reached through this wrapper and through no other call in the
# module, so a wrapper that never matches is that arm taken out of the chain,
# the same way the two graves were freed above. Answering 1 is what test_synch
# answers when the mark it is asked about is not there.
rule ZZtest_synch20_ZZstring77 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end


# ---- a word-final r, which Central Catalan does not say -------------------

# passar is [p @ s a], carrer [k @ R e], amor [@ m u]; clar, cor and mar keep
# theirs. What tells the two apart is not the ending but the word: an r at the
# end goes when there is a second vowel behind it, which is why every one of
# the three that keep it is a syllable long.
#
# The letters are where this can be asked, because a letter rule is handed the
# run it is reading and the scan will walk either way from it. What cannot be
# done there is drop the r outright: assign_span_stress decides between the
# last syllable and the one before it by looking at the last phone, and a word
# that ended in a vowel would take the stress a syllable back -- passar came
# out [p a s @], which is a different word. So the r becomes the phone
# nothing here can reach instead: the word still ends in a consonant when
# the stress is placed. Once it is, st_phnol.up's cat_drop_mute takes the
# phone out again, and the vowel is the last sound of its word from there
# on -- passar comes out sample for sample as passa` would.

# Whether the r the letter rule is reading is the last sound of its word.
# Three things count as the end: nothing to the right at all, something that
# is not a letter, or an s that is itself the last -- carrers is [k @ R e s]
# and dinars [d i n a s], because the plural is added to a word that had
# already lost its r.
rule cat_word_end takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # a letter after it?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # an s?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # and nothing after that?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# One step of the walk leftwards over the letters: nought where the step
# found a vowel and one where it did not. A test that matches leaves the scan
# where it was, so the letter has to be stepped over by hand; advance_tok is
# what does that, and it answers the other way round from the tests, nought
# for a step taken.
#
# A step that has run out of word does not move, so asking again asks about
# the same place and answers the same. That is what lets the caller below
# repeat this a fixed number of times without having to be told to stop.
rule cat_vowel_step takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # still inside the word?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1                                # a vowel?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call advance_tok
  answer 1
end

# Whether there is another vowel behind the one the caller has stopped on.
# Written out rather than looped: the notation's while is a backtracking loop
# and wants a choice point, which a bare rule has no business planting -- the
# ones around the call belong to the letter rule that made it. Eight steps is
# further than any Catalan word puts two vowels apart; constructor has three
# letters between its last two and transgressor three.
rule cat_more_vowels_left takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# The phone a dropped r becomes, laid down between the two ends the letter
# rule was handed. The same shape as the schwa's inserter above.
rule cat_insert_mute takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_mute 0
end



# ---- the o that is open, and the l that takes an r with it ---------------

# Whether the scan has come to the end of the word from where it already
# stands. It is the tail of cat_word_end asked on its own: nothing more, or
# an s that is itself the last thing, which is the plural -- cargols is
# [k @ r G O l s] and cors [k O r s], and both are the same word with an s
# on it.
rule cat_letter_end takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # another letter?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # an s?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # and nothing after that?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# One step of the walk leftwards over the letters, looking for the one that
# carries the written accent: nought where the step found one and one where
# it did not. It is cat_vowel_step with the accent asked about instead of the
# vowel, and it stops at the word boundary the same way, since what stands
# before the first letter is not a letter.
rule cat_accent_step takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # still inside the word?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_5_0                                # does it carry one?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call advance_tok
  answer 1
end

# Whether any letter behind the one the caller has stopped on carries the
# accent. Eight steps, written out for the reason cat_more_vowels_left is:
# the notation's while is a backtracking loop and a bare rule has no business
# planting the choice point one wants.
#
# What it is for is the o before a final l. Catalan writes an accent on a
# word ending in a consonant only where the stress is not on the last
# syllable, so an accent behind the o says the o is not the stressed vowel:
# titol, capitol, consol and idol are [t i t u l], [k @ p i t u l],
# [k O n s u l] and [i D u l], where caragol and Oriol take the open vowel.
# Without this the reduction that would have made them a u never happens,
# because the open o is a phone of its own and no arm of the value chain
# reduces it.
rule cat_accent_left takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_accent_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# Whether the letter after the one being read is an l that ends its word.
# That is where Catalan writes the open o: sol, col, gol, Oriol, caragol,
# espanyol, rossinyol, futbol -- the vowel before a final l is [O] and the
# module had been saying the close one for all of them.
#
# The caller hands over its right-hand end, so the scan is set past the run
# it is reading, which for a vowel is the vowel itself. A test that matches
# steps the scan past what it matched, so the end of the word is asked about
# from after the l.
rule cat_final_l_follows takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1                 # the letter after it
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring69                  # an l?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call cat_letter_end
end

# And whether it is an r that ends its word, which is the other place the
# open o stands: or, cor, por, sor. The r asks one thing more than the l
# does, and apply_span_o_rules asks it -- that no vowel stands behind the o
# -- because a word long enough to lose its r keeps the close vowel: amor is
# [@ m o] and senyor [s @ N o], where or is [O r]. It is the same question
# the r rule asks from the other side, since an r that is still said is one
# with no second vowel behind it, and a said r is what the open o stands
# before.
rule cat_final_r_follows takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1                 # the letter after it
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring94                  # an r?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call cat_letter_end
end


# Whether the o is the one of an ending that says it is open: ola or osa,
# and their plurals oles and oses. escola, cassola, pistola, cosa, rosa and
# nosa all have the open vowel, and none of them writes it, because the
# stress is where the rule would put it anyway. What is asked is the l or the
# s after the o, then an a or an e, then the end of the word by the test the
# final l uses. Every osa is taken, the adjectives in -os with the nouns,
# which is what was asked for.
rule cat_open_o_ending takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1                 # the letter after the o
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring69                  # an l,
  if answer is not 0
    call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                # or an s?
    if answer is not 0
      answer 1
    end
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_a              # then a,
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_e            # or the e of the plural?
    if answer is not 0
      answer 1
    end
  end
  call cat_letter_end                                  # and nothing more
end

# Whether the phone the scan is on is the n a Catalan word ends with, which
# is the interdental's code wearing an n's record. ss_val.up says why.
rule cat_test_final_n takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_final_n
end



# ---- the name of a letter -----------------------------------------------

# What a lone letter is called, where Catalan and Castilian disagree about it.
# rules/constants says which six those are and why the other twenty come out
# right already; u_integ.up says where this is asked from.
#
# A name goes in as characters, which the letter rules then read, so this is
# the same path a number word takes and it asks nothing of the module that
# convert_span_single_digit does not already ask. The letter is looked at
# where the run begins -- setscan_r on the left-hand end is the run's first
# character, the way it is in the digit rules -- and a test that matches
# steps the scan past it, which is why the two cases are asked in that order
# and the insertion loads the pointers again rather than trusting the scan.
#
# Answering 0 says the letter was named here. Anything else is a letter this
# has nothing to say about, and char_name then asks the dictionary as before.
rule cat_char_name takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1                 # the letter itself
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_h                # h is hac, not ache
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigh
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 3 sym cat_name_hac 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_s                # s is essa, not ese
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigs
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_name_essa 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_v                # v is ve, not uve
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigv
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 2 sym cat_name_ve 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_w                # w is ve doble
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigw
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 9 sym cat_name_vedoble 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_x                # x is ics, not ekis
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigx
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 3 sym cat_name_ics 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_y                # y is i grega
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigy
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 8 sym cat_name_igrega 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_cedilla          # c with a cedilla is
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigcedilla     # ce trencada
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 12 sym cat_name_cetrencada 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_j                # jota, with the o open
  if answer is not 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_bigj
  end
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_name_jota 0
    answer answer
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_char_zero             # and a bare nought is
  if answer is 0                                       # zero, not cero
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_num_zero 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring134                 # and the other nine
  if answer is 0                                       # digits, when spelled
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 3 sym cat_num_un 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring135
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 3 sym cat_num_dos 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring136
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_num_tres 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring137
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 6 sym cat_num_quatre 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring138
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_num_cinc 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring139
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_num_sis 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring141
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_num_set 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring142
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 4 sym cat_num_vuit 0
    answer answer
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring143
  if answer is 0
    call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
    call insert_2pt_s 1 3 sym cat_num_nou 0
    answer answer
  end
  answer 1
end

# ---- j and g before e or i: the affricate, except between vowels ---------

# Catalan says the voiced postalveolar two ways. Between vowels it is the
# fricative on its own -- dijous is [d i Z o w s], pluja [p l u Z @], llegeix
# [L @ Z e y S]. At the start of a word and after a consonant it is the
# affricate: jove is [d Z o B @], gener [d Z @ n e], Girona [d Z i r o n @],
# penjat [p @ N d Z a t], menjar [m @ N d Z a], angel [a N d Z @ l]. The
# fricative at the start of a word was what a listener heard as a zh and not
# as Catalan, and Central Catalan does say the affricate there. It is the
# same affricate tg and tj already spell in metge and platja, which is why
# the bytes it lays down are the ones rules/constants already holds for
# those.
#
# The letter is where this can be asked, because a letter rule is handed the
# run it is reading and the scan will walk either way from it. At the start
# of a word the leftward scan does not fail: it lands on the boundary, and a
# boundary is not a vowel, so the start of a word takes the affricate without
# a test of its own. What the rule cannot see is the word before, so la jove
# takes the affricate too, where a careful speaker keeps the fricative across
# the boundary; most Barcelona speech hardens it there as well.

# Lay the voiced postalveolar down between the two ends a letter rule was
# handed: one phone after a vowel and two everywhere else.
rule cat_insert_zz takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_l__1 arg 1
  if answer is 0
    call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1                                # a vowel before it?
    if answer is 0
      call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
      call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_zz 0
      answer answer
    end
  end
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 2 sym cat_ph_dzz 0
end


# Whether what follows the letter the scan has been set to is the sound j and
# g before e and i say. An n in front of it is the palatal nasal rather than
# the alveolar one -- penjat is [p @ N d Z a t] and mengen [m e N d Z @ n],
# which is the same assimilation Castilian makes in front of its own ch and
# which Catalan spelling reaches through two more letters.
#
# A test that matches steps the scan past what it matched, so the front
# vowel is asked about the letter after the g; one that fails leaves it where
# it was, so the tests can be asked one after another.
rule cat_palatal_follows takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring64                  # a j?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring54                  # or a g,
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call cat_front_follows                               # with e or i after it?
end

# Whether the letter the scan is on is a front vowel, which is what makes a
# c or a g soft: e or i, plain or with either accent. The accented ones are
# letters of their own here and were not being asked about, so gènere came
# out with a hard g and precís with a hard c. Answering 0 is a match.
rule cat_front_follows takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring77                  # e
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring109                 # i
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_egrave         # e with a grave
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_eacute         # e with an acute
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_iacute         # i with an acute
end
